INT. GENERATION SHIP "TERRA FIRMA" - HYDROPONICS BAY 4 - NIGHT CYCLE

Rows of silver trays under violet grow-lamps. Pale lettuce, tomato vines climbing steel trellises. A mist-sprayer hisses at intervals. The deck hums with the slow breath of recycled air.

CAPTAIN ADAEZE NWOSU, 50s, stands at a control panel, uniform jacket unbuttoned, tired eyes. Beside her, DR. MIKKEL HAVERLAND, 40s, botanist, a smear of soil on his sleeve. SOLENNE ARCHAMBAULT, 30s, chief engineer, grease to the elbow, leans on a trellis. YUSUF ADEYEMI, 20s, junior technician, perches on an upturned bucket, a tablet on his knees.

ADAEZE
Dr. Haverland, how long?

MIKKEL
Three weeks of yield. Four if we ration.

SOLENNE
Four if we cut the second bay.

YUSUF
We can't cut the second bay.

SOLENNE
We can cut whatever we like.

YUSUF
It's the tomatoes.

SOLENNE
Yusuf, nobody is dying for a tomato.

ADAEZE
Quiet. All of you.

A beat. The mist hisses. Far off, a pump thunks.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
The blight is in Bay Three. If it crosses to Four, we lose the whole deck. I don't want a vote. I want a plan.

MIKKEL
The only plan is isolation. We seal Three, burn it.

SOLENNE
Burn it? That's six hundred kilos of food.

MIKKEL
Contaminated food.

YUSUF
We can't burn it, ma'am. The second generation was born in that bay.

ADAEZE
What?

YUSUF
The children. They used to play there. Before the sealing.

SOLENNE
Yusuf, that's sentiment.

YUSUF
It's memory.

ADAEZE
Enough. Mikkel, can you save any of it?

MIKKEL
Maybe a tenth. If I graft cuttings now.

SOLENNE
A tenth is nothing.

MIKKEL
A tenth is seed stock. It's the future.

SOLENNE
It's a gamble.

MIKKEL
So is everything on this ship.

YUSUF
So we graft?

ADAEZE
We graft. And we burn the rest.

SOLENNE
Captain.

ADAEZE
Solenne, prepare the incinerator.

SOLENNE
It's not designed for biomass.

ADAEZE
Then redesign it by morning.

SOLENNE
(muttering)
Forty hours without sleep and she wants miracles.

ADAEZE
I heard that.

SOLENNE
Good.

Adaeze almost smiles. She rubs her eyes with the heels of her hands. Looks out across the long rows of pale green.

ADAEZE
Mikkel, take Yusuf. Cut what you can save.

MIKKEL
Yes, Captain.

YUSUF
Can I keep one tomato?

ADAEZE
One.

YUSUF
For the kids.

ADAEZE
For the kids.

Yusuf rises. Mikkel slings a case of tools over his shoulder. The two of them move off down the aisle. Solenne watches them go.

SOLENNE
You know it'll be the last tomato.

ADAEZE
I know.

SOLENNE
And you let him keep it.

ADAEZE
Somebody should.

The lamps flicker, steady again. The mist hisses. From somewhere deep in the bay, a child's laughter echoes, a recording, looping, from a speaker long forgotten.

Adaeze closes her eyes.
